---
import { Code } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import project from '../../.generated/project.json';
import example from '../../examples/vec.c?raw';
const paths = [
  {
    number: '01',
    title: 'Run your first check',
    description: 'Install WeaveC, build a program, and watch a bad access stop with a report.',
    href: '/getting-started/first-check/',
    label: 'GET STARTED',
  },
  {
    number: '02',
    title: 'Bring your codebase',
    description: 'Build with CC=weavec-cc under Make, CMake or a configure script.',
    href: '/guides/build-integration/',
    label: 'INTEGRATE',
  },
  {
    number: '03',
    title: 'Adopt it',
    description: 'Run your tests under the guarded build, fix what traps, and measure the cost.',
    href: '/guides/adoption/',
    label: 'GO DEEPER',
  },
];
---

<div class="home">
  <section class="home-hero" aria-labelledby="_top">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <a class="release-pill" href="/project/releases/"
        ><span class="pulse-dot"></span> WEAVEC v{project.version}<span class="pill-divider">/</span
        > EARLY RELEASE <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
      >
      <h1 id="_top">C, with more<br /><span>certainty.</span></h1>
      <p class="hero-description">
        Memory safety for the C you already write. WeaveC is a drop-in C compiler that guards every
        memory access and stops the program at the first bad one, with a precise report.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button-primary" href="/getting-started/installation/"
          >Get started <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a
        ><a class="button-secondary" href="/getting-started/introduction/"
          >Explore the docs <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
        >
      </div>
      <div class="hero-footnote"><span>BUILT ON CLANG + LLVM</span><span>OPEN SOURCE</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-demo" aria-label="WeaveC stops a read past the end of a heap block">
      <div class="demo-label">
        <span class="demo-dot"></span> STOP AT THE FIRST BAD ACCESS <span>C17</span>
      </div>
      <Code code={example} lang="c" title="vec.c" mark="return v->data[i];" />
      <div class="report-output">
        <div>
          <span class="trap-marker">TRAP</span><span>./vec 4</span>
        </div><p>weavec: heap-buffer-overflow at vec.c:9:43: read of 4 bytes</p><span
          class="report-note">↳ 0 bytes after the 16-byte heap object</span
        >
      </div>
      <div class="demo-caption">
        <span aria-hidden="true">✓</span> No source changes. Built with weavec-cc, as Clang builds it.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="principle-strip" aria-label="WeaveC principles">
    <span>YOUR C SOURCE</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><strong>GUARD EVERY ACCESS</strong
    ><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><strong>STOP AT THE FIRST BAD ONE</strong><span
      aria-hidden="true">→</span
    ><span>CLANG + LLVM</span>
  </div>
  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="start-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">A CLEAR PATH FORWARD</span><h2 id="start-title">
          Start small. Build confidence.
        </h2>
      </div><p>
        From one source file to a whole build.<br />Choose the next step for your project.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="path-grid">
      {
        paths.map((entry) => (
          <a class="path-card" href={entry.href}>
            <>
              <div class="card-top">
                <>
                  <span>{entry.label}</span>
                  <span class="card-number">{entry.number}</span>
                </>
              </div>
              <h3>{entry.title}</h3>
              <p>{entry.description}</p>
              <span class="card-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                ↗
              </span>
            </>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="home-section model-section" aria-labelledby="model-title">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">A GUARANTEE YOU CAN REASON ABOUT</span><h2 id="model-title">
        Same code.<br />Same build.
      </h2><p>
        Point CC at weavec-cc and keep your sources, your build and your ABI. Every access is
        checked against the object its pointer came from, and freed memory is reused only once
        nothing points to it.
      </p><a class="text-link" href="/reference/guarantees/"
        >Read the safety guarantees <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a
      >
    </div>
    <div class="model-list">
      <div>
        <span>01</span><section>
          <h3>Guard every access</h3><p>
            Loads, stores, memory intrinsics and library calls are checked, however far an index
            jumps, and however long ago a block was freed.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div><div>
        <span>02</span><section>
          <h3>Keep your toolchain</h3><p>
            weavec-cc is Clang's driver: configure scripts, Make and CMake see a C compiler, and
            code written to be clean under AddressSanitizer is clean under WeaveC.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div><div>
        <span>03</span><section>
          <h3>Know the boundary</h3><p>
            A guard is removed only where a local rule proves it redundant, and the ledger lists
            each access. WEAVEC_UNSAFE marks the code you choose to leave unguarded.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="reference-band" aria-labelledby="reference-title">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">WHEN YOU NEED THE DETAILS</span><h2 id="reference-title">
        Keep the reference close.
      </h2>
    </div><div class="reference-links">
      <a href="/reference/cli/">Command line <span>Find the right flag ↗</span></a><a
        href="/reference/guarantees/">Safety guarantees <span>Know what is caught ↗</span></a
      ><a href="/reference/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting <span>Read a report ↗</span></a><a
        href="/rfcs/">Design RFCs <span>Read the reasoning ↗</span></a
      >
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="scope-note">
    <span class="scope-icon" aria-hidden="true">i</span><p>
      <strong>Early software. Explicit guarantees.</strong> The guarantee covers every guarded access,
      under named assumptions; the scope page lists what it does not catch. <a
        href="/reference/guarantees/">Read the scope and limits →</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
</div>
